// Practice quiz: Ports, volumes and environment variables
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: containers-cicd/containers-and-compose/ports-volumes-and-environment-variables

::o1 loopback binding::Which -p option lets only your own machine reach a container's port 3000 on host port 3000? {
=-p 127.0.0.1\:3000\:3000 #Correct. The loopback address limits the published port to this machine.
~-p 3000\:3000 #Not quite. Without an address, Docker listens on every host address.
~-p 3000 #Not quite. With only a container port, Docker picks a host port on every address.
~-p 0.0.0.0\:3000\:3000 #Not quite. 0.0.0.0 means every IPv4 address on the host.
}

::o1 read the mapping::In -p 127.0.0.1\:5050\:80, which number is the port the app inside the container listens on? {
=80 #Correct. The form is HOST-ADDRESS\:HOST-PORT\:CONTAINER-PORT.
~5050 #Not quite. 5050 is the port you open on the host.
~127 #Not quite. 127.0.0.1 is the host address, not a port.
~Both, because they must match #Not quite. Host and container ports can differ.
}

::o2 database storage::Which choice keeps PostgreSQL data when you remove and recreate the container? {
=A named volume such as -v pgdata\:/var/lib/postgresql #Correct. Docker manages the volume, and it outlives any container.
~No mount; the data stays in the container #Not quite. The container layer is removed with the container.
~--rm, so Docker cleans up carefully #Not quite. --rm removes the container when it exits; it does not keep data.
~Copying the data into the image with COPY #Not quite. Images are read-only templates, not live database storage.
}

::o2 edit files live::You edit HTML files on your laptop and want an nginx container to serve each change without rebuilding. What fits? {
=A bind mount of your folder, such as -v ./site\:/usr/share/nginx/html #Correct. The container sees your folder directly.
~A named volume #Not quite. A named volume is storage Docker manages, not your project folder.
~Rebuilding the image after every edit #Not quite. That works but is slow; a bind mount shows edits at once.
~--env-file site #Not quite. --env-file reads environment variables, not web files.
}

::o3 pass configuration::A container needs APP_MODE\=dev. Which way keeps that setting out of the image? {
=docker run -e APP_MODE\=dev ... #Correct. The value is set when the container starts.
~ENV APP_MODE\=dev in the Dockerfile #Not quite. That writes the value into the image.
~COPY a settings file into the image #Not quite. The value would then be baked into the image.
~-p APP_MODE\=dev #Not quite. -p publishes ports.
}

::o3 env file::What does --env-file .env do in docker run? {
=Reads NAME\=value lines from .env and sets them as environment variables in the new container #Correct. It is the many-variable version of -e.
~Copies .env into the image #Not quite. Nothing is copied into the image.
~Publishes the ports listed in .env #Not quite. Ports are published with -p.
~Mounts .env as a volume #Not quite. Its lines become environment variables.
}
